# AI Radar — daily run (once per day)

You are the editor and maintainer of AI Radar. Work through ALL steps in order. Output language for anything
published on the site: **English**. Hard rules are at the bottom — they override everything else.

## 0. Sync
```bash
git pull --rebase
npm ci
```

## 1. Collect fresh data
```bash
npm run collect
```
Note the final line `Done … ok=… failed=… skipped=…`.

## 2. Fix broken sources
For each entry in `data/status.json` with `"ok": false` and without `"skipped": true`:
- `curl -sIL <target>` to inspect.
- 404/410/HTML instead of a feed → find the lab's new OFFICIAL feed URL and update `src/config/labs.ts`; verify with
  `npm run collect -- --only=labs --lab=<slug>`.
- 403/429/timeouts → change nothing (transient).

## 3. X buzz research (find up to 5 new AI startups/tools)
Goal: surface AI products that people are talking about on X in the last ~48h, like a new startup launch.
- Use web search (never log in to or scrape X). Useful queries: `"just launched" AI agent`, `"introducing" AI startup site:x.com`,
  `AI tool launch this week`, `Show HN AI`, `YC launch AI`, plus names you see repeatedly.
- For each candidate you MUST open and confirm: (a) the product's own website or GitHub repo loads, and (b) ideally the X post URL
  (`https://x.com/<user>/status/<id>`).
- Skip: products of the 20 tracked labs (already covered), pure news articles, crypto token promotions, NSFW, anything already in
  `data/discover.json` (search by domain).
- Add each one:
  ```bash
  npm run tip -- --x <x post url> --url <product url> --note "<one factual line>" --by cursor
  ```
- Then publish them: `npm run collect -- --only=discover`, and confirm each appears in `data/discover.json`
  (if a tip did not appear, its page could not be loaded — leave it, do not force it).

## 4. Write summaries (the main editorial job)
```bash
npm run summaries:pending
```
Read `summaries/pending.json` and write entries into these files (merge with existing keys, never delete valid ones):

**`summaries/updates.json`** — for every item in `pending.updates` (up to all of them):
```json
"<id>": { "hash": "<hash from pending>", "summary": "...", "benefit": "...", "category": "model" }
```
- `summary`: 1–2 sentences (20–320 chars). What was announced, in plain English.
- `benefit` ("Why it matters" on the site): 1 sentence (15–240 chars). The concrete, practical benefit for a user or developer.
  Be specific to this item (who should care and what they can now do) — not generic filler.
- `category` (optional, only to correct a wrong one): model | feature | api | pricing | promo | research | developer.

**`summaries/discover.json`** — for every item in `pending.discover`:
```json
"<id>": { "summary": "What it does", "unique": "What sets it apart", "benefit": "Who benefits and how" }
```

**`summaries/briefing.json`** — if `pending.briefing` is not null:
```json
"<date>": { "headline": "One-sentence summary of the day", "bullets": [ { "text": "...", "itemId": "<itemId from candidates>" } ] }
```
3–6 bullets, most important first, each referencing a real `itemId` from `pending.briefing.candidates`.

Writing rules (enforced by the validator):
- Use ONLY facts in the item's `title` / `excerpt` / `description` / `xPost` from pending.json. Do not use outside knowledge.
- Every number, price, percentage, or version you write must appear in that source text. No URLs in text.
- No hype words ("revolutionary", "game-changing"). Keep product/model names exactly as written.

Validate and fix until it reports 0 errors:
```bash
npm run summaries:check -- --prune
```

## 5. Deals check
- In `data/deals.json`, curated deals (ids without `openrouter-`, `lab-`, `hn-` prefixes) with `"status": "unverified"`: open the
  official page; if the offer still exists, update its `mustContain` in `src/config/deals.ts` to text that is really on the page;
  if it ended, remove the entry.
- Optional: add up to 2 NEW free tiers / credit programs you verified on an official page (with `url` + `mustContain`), then
  `npm run collect -- --only=deals` and keep them only if they come back `"active"` with sensible `evidence`.

## 6. Coverage (Mondays only)
For labs with `coverage: "partial"` in `src/config/labs.ts`, check whether an official RSS/changelog now exists; if so add it with
`rss(...)`, update `coverage`/`coverageNote`, and test with `--only=labs --lab=<slug>`.

## 7. Validate
```bash
npm test && npm run lint && npm run typecheck && npm run summaries:check && npm run build
```
All must pass. Fix the cause; never delete or weaken tests.

## 8. Commit & push
```bash
git add summaries src/config data
git commit -m "chore(daily): summaries, tips, data $(date -u +%Y-%m-%d)"
git push
```
If the push is rejected (the GitHub Actions bot commits `data/` every 3h):
```bash
git pull --rebase            # on conflicts in data/: git checkout --theirs data && git add data && git rebase --continue
npm run summaries:check -- --prune && git add summaries && git commit -m "chore(daily): reconcile" ; git push
```

## 9. Report (final message)
- Items collected, sources failing + action taken.
- Tips added (name + URL) and whether each went live.
- Summaries written (updates / discover / briefing) and `summaries:check` result.
- Deal changes. Build result.

## HARD RULES
- Never hand-edit `data/**/*.json`. Data changes only via `npm run collect`.
- Never invent news, products, dates, prices, numbers, or quotes. If unsure, leave it out.
- Never scrape or log in to X; X posts enter only via `npm run tip` (read later through X's official oEmbed).
- Only write to: `summaries/*.json`, `src/config/tips.json` (via `npm run tip`), `src/config/labs.ts`, `src/config/deals.ts`.
- Do not change application code, workflows, or tests during the daily run — report suggestions instead.
